<p><strong>Autopsy Guideline</strong></p>
<p><strong>Introduction to Autopsy</strong></p>
<p>Autopsy is the most popular open-source digital forensics tool and is
used to analyze evidence collected from digital devices. It is basically
the GUI version of The Sleuth Kit (TSK), which is an excellent digital
forensics command-line suite.</p>
<p>In other words, it provides users with an easy-to-use graphical
interface through which The Sleuth Kit can be used to perform various
investigations such as examining files, recovering deleted information,
browsing through Internet activity, indexing keywords, and creating
visual timelines of activities.</p>
<p><strong>1.Download &amp; Installation</strong></p>
<p>Autopsy is primarily designed for Windows environments (as a native
64-bit or 32-bit application), though it can also be configured for
Linux and macOS.</p>
<p><strong>Download the Software</strong></p>
<p>Always fetch the installer directly from the official repository to
ensure security and integrity:</p>
<p><strong>Official Website:</strong> <a href="https://www.autopsy.com/download/">https://www.autopsy.com/download/</a></p>
<p><strong>Alternative Source:</strong> <a href="https://www.sleuthkit.org/autopsy/">https://www.sleuthkit.org/autopsy/</a></p>
<p><strong>System Requirements for Autopsy Forensic Tool</strong></p>
<p><strong>Operating System (OS)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Minimum:</strong> Windows 10 / 11 (64-bit) or Kali Linux
/ Ubuntu (64-bit).</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Maximum / Optimal:</strong> Windows 10 / 11 (64-bit) or a
dedicated 64-bit Linux Server.</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Processor (CPU)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Minimum:</strong> Dual-core or Quad-core processor (e.g.,
Intel Core i3 / i5 or AMD Ryzen 3). Needs a clock speed of at least 2.0
GHz to process basic modules without freezing.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Maximum / Optimal</strong>: Multi-core processor (8 to
16+ Cores, e.g., Intel Core i7 / i9, AMD Ryzen 9, or Intel
Xeon).</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Memory (RAM) Allocation</strong></p>
<p>Autopsy is built on the NetBeans platform and runs heavily on
Java.</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>At Minimum (8 GB):</strong> Autopsy will run, but you
will need to limit the number of simultaneous ingest modules (e.g.,
running Keyword Search, Hash Lookup, and File Type Analysis all at once
will likely crash the JVM or cause severe lag).</p></li>
<li><p><strong>At Maximum (32 GB+):</strong> Allows you to maximize the
pipelines and comfortably allocate 16 GB+ specifically to the Java heap
size, preventing "Out of Memory" errors during deep indexing.</p></li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Storage Setup (The Ultimate Bottleneck)</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Digital forensics is incredibly I/O intensive. If your project
involves large disk images, consider this structured storage setup:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>The "Good" Setup (Minimum):</strong> Storing your
operating system, the Autopsy case folder, and the digital image on a
single large HDD. (Expect slow ingest speeds).</p></li>
<li><p><strong>The "Best" Setup (Maximum):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Drive 1 (SSD):</strong> Operating System and Autopsy
installation.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Drive 2 (Fast NVMe SSD):</strong> The Autopsy
<strong>Case Output Directory</strong> (where indexes and databases are
built).</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Drive 3 (HDD/SSD):</strong> The <strong>Data Source
Location</strong> (where your raw/E01 images are stored
read-only).</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Ingest Module Configuration</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Minimum Config:</strong> If your system hits the lower
end of the specs, disable resource-heavy ingest modules like
<strong>Optical Character Recognition (OCR)</strong>, <strong>Video
Triage</strong>, and <strong>Solr Keyword Search</strong> unless
absolutely necessary.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Maximum Config:</strong> With a high-end multi-core CPU
and 32GB+ RAM, you can enable all modules simultaneously and increase
the <em>Number of Ingest Threads</em> in Autopsy’s settings to match
your CPU core count.</p></li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>System Setup</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>1.Run the Installer:</strong> Windows setup.</p>
<p>Double-click the downloaded .msi file. Follow the installation wizard
prompts, accept the terms, and choose your installation directory.</p>
<p><strong>2.Configure Central Repository (Optional):</strong> Database
selection.</p>
<p>During setup, you will be asked if you want to configure a Central
Repository. For standard, single-user setups, choose the default
<strong>SQLite</strong> option. Large enterprise teams should opt for
PostgreSQL.</p>
<p><strong>3.Verify Resource Allocation:</strong> Performance check.</p>
<p>Autopsy is resource-heavy. Ensure your system has at least
<strong>8GB of RAM</strong> and plenty of free storage space, as
forensic cases generate massive temporary data folders.</p>
<p><strong>2.Getting Started with Autopsy</strong></p>
<p>When you run Autopsy for the very first time, you would be guided
through the process of creating your first case, adding a disk image to
that case, and running the automatic disk analysis process in Autopsy,
which is called ingesting.</p>
<p><strong>Flow for Autopsy Process</strong></p>
<p>You can always add more images at any point after you have created
the case. Also, you can change the ingest and start the process of
analyzing the disk again.</p>
<p>In this chapter, you will be learning how to</p>
<p>• Create a case</p>
<p>• Add a disk image to a case</p>
<p>• Configure the ingest to analyze the disk images</p>
<p>• Cancel and start the process of ingestion again</p>
<p>• Add a hash database for improved data analysis</p>
<p>This is going to prepare you for moving on with your analysis
process.</p>
<ol type="I">
<li><p><strong>Creating a Case</strong></p></li>
</ol>
<p>Every investigation in Autopsy starts by creating a "Case." Think of
a case as a secure project folder that holds your databases,
configuration settings, and all the data extracted during your
analysis<strong>.</strong></p>
<p><strong>1.Launch the Application:</strong></p>
<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:4.89583in;height:2.525in"><strong>Step 1.</strong> Open
Autopsy. On the initial splash screen, in top left you see case click it
and the New Case icon (represented by a folder with a green plus
sign).</p>
<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:3.73958in;height:2.50833in"></p>
<p>Then fill in all the necessary case information like the case name
and choose a base directory to save all the case data in one place.</p>
<p><strong>2.Name your Project and Set a Destination:</strong></p>
<p><strong>Step 2.</strong> In the New Case Information window:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Case Name: Type a unique name (e.g., Case_001_Laptop). Avoid
special characters.</p></li>
<li><p>Base Directory: Click Browse and select a folder on your storage
drive where Autopsy can save its files.</p></li>
<li><p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:4.20833in;height:2.50709in"><em>Tip:</em> Always ensure
this drive has plenty of free space; forensic extraction files can get
massive! Click Next.</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3.Add Investigator Metadata:</strong></p>
<p><strong>Step 3.</strong> On the Additional Information screen, fill
out your tracking details:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Case Number: Your internal case tracking identifier.</p></li>
<li><p>Examiner Name: Your name or initials.</p></li>
<li><p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:4.25in;height:2.533in"><em>Warning:</em> Fill this out
carefully. Autopsy locks these fields into the database upon creation,
meaning you cannot change or edit your examiner name later.</p></li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Between Additional Information and type of data source one more
section open.</p>
<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:3.25in;height:2.01042in"><strong>4. Select
host:</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Select Generate new host name based on data source name and click
next.</p>
<p><strong>5.Initialize the Database:</strong></p>
<p><strong>Step 4.</strong> Click Finish. Autopsy will take a few
seconds to build the core database structures and will automatically
launch the Add Data Source Wizard.</p>
<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:5.73333in;height:3.60538in"></p>
<p><strong>Now let us add the type of data source. There are various
types to choose from.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Disk Image or VM file:</strong>&nbsp; This includes the image file
which can be an exact copy of a hard drive, media card, or even a
virtual machine.</p>
<p><strong>Local Disk:</strong>&nbsp;This option includes devices like Hard
disk, Pen drives, memory cards, etc.</p>
<p><strong>Logical Files</strong>: It includes the image of any local
folders or files.</p>
<p><strong>Unallocated Space Image File</strong>: They include files
that do not contain any file system and run with the help of the ingest
module.</p>
<p><strong>Autopsy Logical Imager Results:&nbsp;</strong>They include the
data source from running the logical imager.</p>
<p><strong>XRY Text Export:</strong>&nbsp;This includes the data source from
exporting text files from XRY,</p>
<p>Now let us add the data source. Here we have a previously created
image file, so we will add the location of that file.</p>
<ol start="2" type="I">
<li><p><strong>Adding a Disk Image</strong></p></li>
</ol>
<p>Now that your case file container exists, you need to point Autopsy
to the actual digital evidence you want to analyze.</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><p><strong>Select Host:</strong> On the first screen of the data
source wizard, leave the default setting: <em>"Generate new host name
based on data source name"</em> and click Next.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Select Source Type:</strong> Choose Disk Image or VM File
(this is used for raw copies of hard drives or USBs like .dd, .raw, or
forensic formats like .E01). Click Next.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Path Selection:</strong> Click the Browse button next to
the "Path" field. Locate and select your forensic image file on your
computer.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Time Zone Settings:</strong> Ensure the drop-down time
zone matches the time zone of the device you are investigating. This
ensures your timeline stays accurate. Click Next.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>Now let us add the data source. Here we have a previously created
image file, so we will add the location of that file.</p>
<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:5.1in;height:3.10694in"></p>
<p>Next, the system will prompt you to&nbsp;configure the Ingest Module.</p>
<ol start="3" type="I">
<li><p><strong>Configuring Disk Analysis (Ingest
Modules)</strong></p></li>
</ol>
<p>Before Autopsy actually loads the image, it will show you the Ingest
Modules screen. Ingest modules are automated background tools that scan
the evidence for specific artifacts so you don't have to look for them
manually.</p>
<p><strong>Beginner Checklist</strong>: If you are a beginner running
your first analysis, click Select All to activate the core engines.</p>
<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:6.5in;height:4.07639in"></p>
<p>Below, you will find the contents of the Ingest module:</p>
<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:6.5in;height:4.83681in"></p>
<p>Once your modules are checked, click Next, and then click Finish. The
image will load, and a progress bar will appear in the bottom right
corner showing that data analysis has started.</p>
<ol start="4" type="I">
<li><p><strong>Using a Hash Database</strong></p></li>
</ol>
<p>A Hash Database is a list of unique cryptographic signatures (MD5 or
SHA-256 fingerprints) of known files. Using one allows Autopsy to
automatically identify and filter files for you.</p>
<p><strong>To configure and connect a hash database or data sets to your
workflow:</strong></p>
<p><strong>How to set it up:</strong></p>
<ol type="1">
<li><p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:3.03333in;height:2.14893in">Go to the top menu bar and
click Tools &gt; Options.</p></li>
<li><p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:3.24167in;height:1.55556in">Select the Hash Databases tab
on the left.</p></li>
<li><p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:3.45833in;height:1.65903in">Click the Import Database
button.</p></li>
<li><p>Click Browse to point to your downloaded hash set (such as the
NIST NSRL database).</p></li>
<li><p>Choose its classification type:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Known:</strong> Files matching this list are hidden from
your view because they are safe, standard files (like notepad.exe),
saving you time.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Known Bad:</strong> If a file on the disk matches this
list, Autopsy will immediately flag it and generate an alert in the user
interface.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li><p>Check the box that says Send Ingest Alerts so you get immediate
pop-ups if a match is found. Click OK.</p></li>
</ol>
<p><strong>3: Exploring Analyzed Data</strong></p>
<p>Autopsy organizes your workspace into a highly efficient Three-Pane
Layout. To investigate effectively, a beginner must understand how data
flows sequentially from left to right across these panels.</p>
<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:6.5in;height:2.89444in"></p>
<p>Data Source information displays basic metadata. At the bottom, we
display its detailed analysis. You can extract it one after the
other.</p>
<ol type="i">
<li><p><strong>Using the Data Explorer (Left Panel)</strong></p></li>
</ol>
<p>The Data Explorer is your navigation tree. It organizes everything
found on the disk image into logical folders. As a beginner, focus on
these three primary root nodes:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Data Sources:</strong> This shows you the actual file
system structure of the target device. You can drill down into
directories just like using Windows File Explorer (e.g., browsing
directly into C:\Users\Username\Documents).</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Views:</strong> Autopsy automatically organizes files by
their attributes here, regardless of where they are hidden on the hard
drive:</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Investigating Deleted Files via the Views Tree</strong></p>
<ol type="A">
<li><p><strong>Navigating to Deleted Files</strong></p></li>
</ol>
<p>Once your Data Source has finished ingesting (or while it is
running), you can jump straight into checking for deleted data.</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><p>Look at the <strong>Data Explorer</strong> (the left-hand panel
tree).</p></li>
<li><p>Locate and expand the <strong>Views</strong> root node by
clicking the arrow next to it.</p></li>
<li><p>Click on the <strong>Deleted Files</strong> folder to expand it.
You will see two options:</p></li>
</ol>
<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="width:6.5in;height:2.52569in"></p>
<p><strong>All:</strong> Aggregates absolutely every deleted file
structure Autopsy managed to parse out of the data source.</p>
<p><strong>File System:</strong> Focuses strictly on files that were
deleted but whose structural file-system metadata remains clean enough
to map back to their original folders.</p>
<ol start="2" type="A">
<li><p><strong>Reviewing and Analyzing Deleted Items</strong></p></li>
</ol>
<p>When you click on <strong>All</strong>, a complete directory of
deleted items fills the <strong>Result Viewer</strong> (top-right
panel).</p>
